--TEST--
A `Core`-owned generic class takes exactly the type arguments its roster declares, so one list is right for one collection and wrong for the other — `rule:types/grammar`, spec § 9
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/grammar` makes "which `new` target may carry a `<...>`" a resolution
// question, and `nvs_stdlib::registry::GENERIC_CLASSES` is the answer. It is
// read positionally: nothing but the declared arity tells `ObjectSet<T>` from
// `ObjectMap<K, V>` here, so the two mistakes below are one diagnostic with
// two different counts in it. A target off the roster gets E0441 instead,
// because user-declared generic classes stay deferred.
class Tag {}
var $set = new Core\ObjectSet<Tag, int>();
var $map = new Core\ObjectMap<Tag>();
echo "never reached", "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0442]: `Core\ObjectSet` takes 1 type argument(s), not 2
%A
error[E0442]: `Core\ObjectMap` takes 2 type argument(s), not 1
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
